**Chapter 10.3 『The Sprouting of Light』**

---

Whenever she stepped into the royal castle, Felt always felt a sensation akin to breaking through a thin, filmy layer of resistance.

Before the Royal Selection even began, to Felt—who grew up in the slums of the royal capital—the Lugnica Royal Castle had always been a symbol of complete isolation, something that existed on the very edge of her vision yet had absolutely nothing to do with her own life.

A place that existed, but with which she would never be involved. A hazy, intangible, mirage-like thing that felt worthless to even acknowledge. That was the impression she had.

So even now, every time she had the opportunity to enter the castle as a Royal Selection candidate, she still felt that same sense of unreality, as if she were stepping into a dream or an illusion.

That feeling—was...

"—Lady Felt?"

Hearing her name, Felt, who had stopped walking, raised her head.

She met the blue eyes of her knight, who had his hand on the door and had turned back to look at her, with her own crimson gaze. Snorting lightly, she gave a short reply, "Nothin'."

Yeah, it's nothing. No one can stop our progress now.

"Let's go."

With that declaration, she steeled herself and broke through the thin layer of resistance, moving forward.

As she stepped through the door opened by her nodding knight—Reinhard—the place beyond was, as usual, crowded with the presence of many people and filled with an abnormal tension.

It was a space where all manner of wills clashed chaotically, becoming exchanged as glances and words. However, the moment Felt set foot inside, those scattered wills unintentionally became unified.

Namely, into an impudent will to size Felt up, all for the sake of the kingdom's future.

"Eh, whatever. I ain't bothered. When I meet someone, I'm in that kinda mood pretty often too."

In this regard, regardless of the Royal Selection, this was perhaps Felt's way of dealing with the world, or a bit of street smarts from surviving the slums. Gauging whether the other person was useful or unnecessary, what they might be thinking behind the scenes—it had become a deeply ingrained habit.

In fact, even after becoming a Royal Selection candidate, this ingrained habit had helped her. After all, she was now inundated with opportunities to meet people on an incomparable scale to her slum days and had to discern their intentions. If she'd had to rebuild that sense of values from scratch, she couldn't imagine how much more difficult it would have been.

Though—

"There're some folks like Emilia-neechan who have nothin' to do with all that."

Emilia, a fellow candidate who miraculously managed to remain completely unguarded. Felt thought that the reason she could stay like that was likely due to the extraordinary efforts of her knight, Natsuki Subaru, and Otto, who was accompanying her this time as well.

But on the other hand, Felt's assessment was that neither the Royal Selection nor the environment surrounding it was so easy that one could get through it being completely sheltered and protected all the time. Even Emilia, being who she was, possessed a sharp eye for people and the intuition to sense their motives.

And yet, she maintained that attitude, which made things somewhat tricky for Felt as well.

"In the end, we ended up becomin' friends."

Looking back on the decision she herself had just made, Felt let out a deep sigh. She didn't regret accepting Emilia's proposal, but she also felt her future path had become uncertain again. She never had any intention of relying on underhanded means or schemes in the first place, but if they'd been properly hostile rival camps, it would have been easier to make judgments without holding back. If the other side just had a one-sided goodwill towards her, only they would be at a disadvantage.

Wasn't that essentially Anastasia's basic strategy? Felt had even speculated that might have been the real reason Anastasia invited the other camps to Pristella. —Unfortunately, that scheme seemed to have been shattered by the whole "becoming friends with Emilia after the Royal Selection" development.

Anyway—

"It's fine. I don't hate it. It's been a while since I've gotten these sizing-up looks aimed at me, personally."

It wasn't a bluff. Twisting the corner of her mouth, Felt accepted the attention directed at her like that.

In the throne room she had entered—the place where the start of the Royal Selection had been declared—many people related to Lugnica's royal castle had gathered, including the figures of the 'Sages of the Council' who held the kingdom's central political authority.

She had spoken with them the other day too, when she reported the achievements of Meili Portroute—who had touted her own value by claiming she could guide the way to the 'Sage's' tower regarding the incident at the Augria Sand Dunes and the Pleiades Watchtower—but the atmosphere was distinctly different from back then.

It was a gaze directed at Felt, the look reserved for heretics and outsiders—when she first participated in the Royal Selection, she had picked a fight with everyone present. It was close to the bewilderment and confusion that had been directed at the stray child from the slums back then.

And then—

"Seems like you guys are the ones responsible for that reason, huh? You don't exactly *look* the part though, do ya?"

Stomping firmly on the red carpet, Felt walked right into the center of the throne room. Squinting one eye, she glared at the man and woman who were already standing there.

"————"

Turning to face Felt were a young man and woman, both around twenty years old.

A gentle-looking man with young grass-colored hair, and a beautiful woman with short, neatly tied purple hair reminiscent of flowers blooming in the rainy season. Despite the location, the two stood dignified, not cowed by the surroundings.

Indeed, they didn't flinch even at Felt's assertive opening salvo. The gentle man gave a wry smile, and the woman fluttered a soft hand, saying, "My, my~"

"Ufufu, that's quite a sudden start, isn't it? And this is our first time meeting properly, too. But my, Felt-chan, you really are prickly just as the rumors say~"
"Rumors, huh? What kinda rumors 'bout me?"
"Small and cute, yet dignified and bold; strong-willed and never backing down a step against anyone, like a man... They say the gap in your charms makes people dizzy~"
"Man, that's some pretty unwanted rumors you got there..."

At Felt's exasperated reply, the woman giggled with a sensual laugh.

Her appearance and atmosphere, with her deep crimson clothes like ripe fruit and a slanted hat of the same color, seemed like that of some secretary or a maid serving a noble. But the combination of her brazen way of closing the distance and her drowsy, sleepy-looking red eyes made her seem elusive in some way.

Frankly, she gave the impression of being a tricky customer, but—

"—Sakura, she is one of the Royal Selection candidates. Stop slipping into your usual, familiar tone. We wouldn't have enough lives to pay for it."
And the gentle man standing beside the woman chided his companion's attitude like that. However, even that remark was tinged with a lighthearted color, not exactly guaranteeing the man's seriousness or sincerity.

In fact, at the gentle man's words, the woman called Sakura kept smiling and said, "Is that so~",

"Are you going to make me out to be the bad guy too, Tee-ga-chan~? I'm already in a precarious position because of that child's whims, you know. If even my only ally says such things, I'll be so disappointed~"
"Then, all the more reason we shouldn't make more enemies. First impressions tend to linger long after. Especially now, when we aren't exactly in a welcomed position... right?"
"Ye~s, ye~s, Tee-ga-chan's argument is the correct one~"
"Good that you understand."

At Sakura, who stuck out her tongue with an insolent air, the gentle man nodded with an exaggerated gesture.

Then, turning once more towards Felt's group, he removed the wide-brimmed hat deeply covering his head and, placing it against his chest over his dark purple coat, said,

"And so, my companion has been terribly rude, Lady Felt. I am Tiga Laureon. This is Sakura Element. Please remember us."

As he spoke, the gentle man—Tiga—made a stylish bow, winking one of his yellow eyes directed at Felt. He was a handsome man whose affected mannerisms suited him.

While taking in the sight of Tiga and Sakura, Felt understood. Without a doubt, these two, who stood out so conspicuously from the castle's atmosphere, were the reason Reinhard had hurriedly brought Felt from the noble's mansion—

"Reinhard, these two are..."
"Yes. —They are the two envoys from the 'Dragon Faith'."

Standing beside Felt, who had stopped walking, Reinhard faced the two in question.

Unintentionally, Felt's group and the two from the 'Dragon Faith' now faced each other in the center of the throne room, becoming the sole focus of attention from everyone present in the castle.

Disregarding all those stares, Felt crossed her arms and glared at Tiga.

"From what I know, the 'Dragon Faith' ain't supposed to get involved in national politics. So why're you here?"
"Whether you'll believe me is questionable, but in truth, this contact is unwelcome for us as well. As you say, the Faith has maintained a stance of keeping its distance from politics. Of course, I'm not saying we have no sense of belonging to the Kingdom."
"Unwelcome, you say? Is that your opinion, knowing you might be able to do somethin' 'bout the state of the candidate, Crusch Karsten?"
"—. That is..."

At Felt's words, Tiga showed a slight flinch. Then, beside him, Sakura covered her mouth with her hand and giggled once more.

At her smile, Felt growled, "Hah?" in her throat, prompting Sakura to tilt her head.

"No, no, Felt-chan has such sharp ears, doesn't she~? That matter was supposed to be a secret within the castle walls, I believe~"
"Tch. Don't underestimate the guy standin' next to me and his hellish hearing. This guy can even hear the sound of my feet getting out of bed at night when I go to the toilet."
"I won't deny it, but that's a misleading way of putting it, Lady Felt."
"Shut up. Don't just stand there grinnin' weakly, be brazen about it. That'll make the other side more intimidated."

The source of the information about the discussions held at the castle involving the 'Dragon Faith' being related to Crusch was Otto, so Felt felt obliged to keep that quiet for now. At this point, it wouldn't really hurt Reinhard to have rumors added about him having hellish hearing or eavesdropping habits. It *was* a fact that he could probably distinguish the sound of a needle dropping a kilometer away.

Anyway—

"—I've never liked the 'Dragon Faith' much."
"My, my~ Does Felt-chan dislike the Faith too? I thought our activities, like those in the slums, had a good influence, you know~"
"Sorry, hate bein' on the receivin' end of charity. I never stood in line for the bread the Faith hands out. Not that I'm sayin' those activities are useless or I hate 'em. What I'm talkin' about right now is you guys supposedly bein' able to save Duke Karsten."
"Does that mean you want to leave a suffering rival camp to their fate~?"
"Ya want me to say it's different that badly? Sadly, I ain't that free."

Sticking out her tongue at Sakura, whose words were sweet but whose jabs were poisonous.

True, Crusch was a rival camp, but given the reason she ended up in her current state... Everyone present at that time carried a sense of guilt about it, and if she could recover, it was only human to want to take any available measures. —However, there was *only one* method that sat extremely poorly with Felt: Crusch borrowing the power of the 'Dragon Faith'.

After all—

"—Duke Crusch Karsten declared during the Royal Selection proceedings. That she would break the covenant made with the 'Dragon' and forsake the peace granted under its protection."
"————"

The one who interjected into Felt's conversation was the long-bearded elder seated among the 'Sages of the Council'—Miklotov McMahon. At the words of one of the Sages, who had finally spoken, Felt let out a small sigh.

"...The whole country relyin' on the 'Dragon' is pathetic, she said. If the one who said that herself ends up borrowing the power of the 'Dragon Faith' that worships it... that just ain't cool."
"Watch your tongue, Lady Felt. Matters of appearances..."
"No, Lady Felt is quite correct. Appearances... namely, the matter of how things look. In that respect, Lady Crusch would suffer an irreparable blow. Although, that is only if she accepts the hand extended to her."

As Felt and Miklotov exchanged words, one of the attending civil officials tried to interject, but it was Miklotov himself who admonished him.

The Sage's added words meant that regarding the proposal from the 'Dragon Faith', the final decision rested with Crusch and her camp.

However—

"—Felix."

Slightly lowering his eyes, Reinhard uttered the name of his friend, the holder of the title of 'Blue', Crusch's number one knight who was currently in the depths of anguish.

Felt had also seen, when she visited to check on Crusch's condition, how that beauty—which could be mistaken for a lovely woman—had been haggard, and how much he had suffered and agonized by Crusch's side. She hadn't heard that Crusch's condition had improved since then. If so, his state of mind was likely the same.

His precious master, continuing to burn in the hellish, virulent flames. If a means to save her was presented, whatever decision the number one knight made, no one could blame him for it.

"Of course, we too have had discussions regarding this proposal from the 'Dragon Faith'. First, we must ascertain whether the content of the proposal is factual. Depending on the outcome..."
"The folks who suffered in Pristella might be saved too. If ya think 'bout that, what we're hung up on is pretty small stuff, right?"
"Hmm. And your acquiescence is..."
"Do ya think I can?"

She understood the logic. She understood the reasoning. But emotional acceptance was a different matter.

Having entered the Royal Selection, Felt intended to keep moving forward to win. But if Priscilla had died in the recent events in the Empire, and now Crusch might drop out because of this... that was completely different from the way Felt wanted victory or defeat to be decided.

If things could be settled that way, how was it any different from Reinhard going on a rampage and rendering all the other candidates unable to compete?

"No one in particular is to blame. If forced to say, all fault lies with the Witch Cult."
"Thinkin' 'bout it now, I wish we'd beaten up that 'Wrath' bastard more before throwin' him in prison. Might've made me feel a little better."

Of course, she knew perfectly well that doing so wouldn't have solved anything and probably wouldn't have eased her frustration. Most of the damage in Pristella was inflicted by 'Gluttony' and 'Lust', and the aftereffects of 'Wrath' were extremely minimal.

Reinhard knew that too, so Felt's grumbled words weren't taken seriously by anyone.

In any case—

"I get the gist of the situation. The Faith brought a treatment, and whether Big Sis Crusch gets saved or not depends on whether her knight accepts it... We got no right to butt into that."

Even with her own thoughts on the matter, the choice belonged only to the people involved.

Having acknowledged that, Felt turned a questioning "So?" to Reinhard beside her, and then to everyone present all at once.

"I get the story up to this point. But why'd ya need to drag me here in a hurry? Sure, an explanation is welcome, but ya know I ain't the only one in the capital—Emilia-neechan's here too. If the explanation's just this, tellin' us together wouldn't make a difference."

This matter could greatly affect the Royal Selection. If so, even more than Felt alone, the information should have been urgently shared with Emilia as well. In such a situation, it was unthinkable that Reinhard would try to outmaneuver Emilia's camp and bring only Felt. It was doubtful Reinhard even had the concept of outmaneuvering others in his mind.

And yet, there *had* to be a reason he brought only Felt here.

"If there's a reason, it's you guys from the Faith, right? But as of now, there ain't nothin' we wanna discuss with the Faith... 'Cept maybe 'bout the 'Holy Dragon'."

Muddling her words slightly and lowering her tone in the latter half—regarding the 'Holy Dragon' Volcanica, whom the 'Dragon Faith' so grandly worshipped, Felt's own perception had only recently changed.

"After all, when I went with Meili to the Pleiades Watchtower, I ended up seein' up close how that empty-headed 'Dragon' and Reinhard went at it..."

The battle between the 'Sword Saint' and the 'Holy Dragon' probably hadn't been in an environment where they could go all out, but it was still an apocalyptic, fierce battle. Through that fight, Felt learned that the 'Holy Dragon' was in a state far from the dignity spoken of in legends, but if asked to elaborate on the details, she'd be at a loss for words.

However, in response to Felt's concern, Tiga shrugged his shoulders.

"No, calling Lady Felt here wasn't our intention. Rather, regarding the matter up until now, the Faith is in a somewhat delicate position."
"Up until now, you mean the stuff about Big Sis Crusch? Delicate how?"
"Well, to put it simply... As the Faith, we hadn't yet come to a clear decision on whether to act to save Duke Crusch Karsten."
"Ahh...?"

Frowning at Tiga's difficult expression and reply, Felt also inadvertently let out a low groan.

It was a baffling statement. She had understood the sequence of events as: the 'Dragon Faith' had a means to restore those affected by the Witch Cult, explicitly stated they could save Crusch with it, and thus made contact with the castle. But Tiga's wording just now undermined that premise.

"But that treatment's already been offered to Big Sis Crusch, right? If the Faith ain't decided, then why is it..."
"Well, you see~... One of our impatient children went ahead on their own~"
"...Went ahead?"
"Ye~s. So~, we also rushed to the castle in a flurry~ and were just in the process of inquiring about the situation from everyone~. It's quite troublesome, isn't it~?"

It was hard to tell how much of Sakura's words to take seriously, as they lacked gravity. However, compared to Sakura, Tiga, who seemed much more reasonable, didn't stop or deny her statement now, merely putting a hand to his forehead as if embarrassed by a family member's disgrace.

If that were true, then the 'Dragon Faith' also had its share of reckless personnel.

But even so—

"In the end, that still doesn't explain why *I* was called."
"Hmm. Regarding that point, we too find it very perplexing. It was precisely because Sir Reinhard was present that we requested Lady Felt to come as well. For you see, in the problem we are facing, Lady Felt is a concerned party."
"————"

*She* was a concerned party in *their* problem. Hearing that, Felt narrowed her crimson eyes and licked her lips with her tongue.

Seemed like she'd gone on quite the detour, but it looked like they were finally getting to the main point.

The reason Felt was called to this place, where the envoys of the 'Dragon Faith'—whose actions were tied to Crusch's fate and which were, by their own admission, unexpected even for them—were present.

That reason was—

"The person from the 'Dragon Faith' who made contact with us alone, as mentioned earlier... is a woman with beautiful golden hair and crimson eyes. Moreover..."

Felt didn't interrupt the words being told by Miklotov.

She wasn't so dull as to not understand what the description of this person, introduced with such obvious importance, signified.

Watching Felt's reaction, Miklotov continued his explanation.

"That person gave her name as **Firuore**—claiming it to be the same name as the daughter of His Royal Highness Ford Lugnica, who disappeared fifteen years ago."


-- 

**-- The daughter of Royal Prince Ford Lugnica, Firuore Lugnica.**

The significance of that name and existence was great for the Kingdom of Lugnica, but it held an especially large, enormous meaning for Felt.

After all, the reason Felt had been forced to participate in the Royal Selection—besides, or perhaps even more than, the fact that the Dragon Jewel embedded in the qualification badge had glowed for her—was the suspicion that *Felt herself* might be that vanished Firuore.

In reality, the infant Firuore disappeared fifteen years ago.

That matched Felt's age, and Felt's hair and eye color matched the characteristics seen in the Lugnica Royal Family. Of course, Felt had never thought of herself as a member of the royal family, nor had she tried to use that to her advantage. —But she wasn't so selfish that she could completely ignore what possibilities those around her were hoping for in her.

Many in the Kingdom of Lugnica saw in Felt the possibility of being the lost royal.

Regardless of Felt's own self-perception, it's human nature that such expectations and envy cannot be easily moved. And that wasn't just in the broad framework of the kingdom's citizens; even the people present at the declaration of the Royal Selection's start—even **Reinhard**—had harbored a fragment of that hope.

Undoubtedly, the fact that there was a possibility she was a surviving royal is what had given Felt, a girl from the slums, her first sword to fight on the stage of the Royal Selection.

And then—

**"—The real, vanished Firuore, huh."**

The situation clicked into place in her chest, and Felt muttered it only inside her mouth.

I see. It made sense that shockwaves ran through the royal castle. Or perhaps, even the fear that Crusch might be saved by the 'Dragon Faith' and consequently drop out of the Royal Selection might have been treated as a trivial matter in the face of this fact.

To that extent, the existence of a nun from the 'Dragon Faith' claiming to be Firuore was that shocking.

**"—Lady Felt."**

Suddenly called by Reinhard standing beside her, Felt held her breath slightly.

It wasn't a surprise call, and yet, Felt failed to catch what emotion was carried in that voice. —No, let's admit it. The definite agitation that had sprouted within Felt had prevented her from catching Reinhard's feelings.

**"————"**

At first, when Reinhard had brought her to the Royal Selection stage, due in part to her forced confinement life moving from the loot cellar to the mansion, Felt could only feel strong resistance and hostility. In the end, she ended up deciding to participate due to the course of events involving Old Man Rom's intrusion, but the very foundation for why Reinhard brought Felt to that stage and promoted her as a candidate in the first place was likely the suspicion that she was a surviving royal—a foundation that was now shaking tremendously.

That had been negated. —No, not negated. It was still just a suspicion. Besides, Felt had always mocked and scorned that possibility until now.

And yet, when it was actually negated before her eyes, a definite agitation shook her heart. It wasn't because she had wanted to be royalty.

It was a disappointment born from Felt's own, helpless shortcoming—the thought of betraying the expectations of those who had hoped she was a member of the royal family.

**"...Pathetic."**

Seeing through the true nature of the sprouting agitation, Felt felt disgusted with herself.

She always acts so brazen and cheeky, putting on airs of being unshakable no matter what happens, but the moment she realized something unexpected—that something she thought she could let go of without issue might be taken away—she hated the sight of herself panicking in such an unsightly way.

Gnashing her teeth at her pathetic self, Felt moved her heavy, stiffened neck and managed to turn towards Reinhard standing beside her.

No matter what face Reinhard was making there—...

**"————"**

The sight that met Felt's eyes as she looked up, steeling herself, were Reinhard's azure eyes looking straight at her, holding not a trace of anxiety or agitation. Let alone disappointment or dismay; there was absolutely none of that.

There was only, purely, an immense trust in Felt, and nothing else.

**"...The hell's with that look?"**
**"I believe I harbor an unwavering loyalty towards you, in my own way, Lady Felt."**
**"—. Tch, shut up."**

Whether he meant it as a joke or was serious, Felt responded to Reinhard's slight smile by jabbing his side with her elbow. She had to. Because, annoyingly, Reinhard's current attitude had lightened the emotions occupying her chest all at once.

**"No matter what happens, I'm me. Ain't no choice but to accept that."**

She had already known that perfectly well, but she needed a clear trigger to firmly accept it. Unintentionally given that trigger by Reinhard, Felt felt displeased but refrained from clicking her tongue.

It was displeasing, but not in a bad way. She'd absolutely never tell Reinhard that, though.

It was right after such a firm resolution had been made within Felt.

**"—Sir Knight Marcos Gildark has returned."**

With that announcement from a guard in the throne room, the expressions of those present suddenly grew tense. As Felt wondered at their reaction, Reinhard brought his mouth close to her ear.

**"It seems Captain Marcos escorted the woman in question to Lady Crusch's side. Regardless of the decision on the treatment..."**
**"A face-to-face meeting, huh. Glad that happened after I'd already made up my mind."**

Responding to Reinhard's whisper like that, Felt took a deep, large breath. Then, with Reinhard standing beside her, she waited for the moment.

And then—...

**"—My, what a large crowd waiting for me. Given the circumstances, I suppose it's only natural."**

Felt heard the person who peeked into the throne room after passing through the doorway voice that impression.

**"That's..."**

The nun from the 'Dragon Faith' who claimed to be Firuore and marched into the royal castle?

Indeed, as described beforehand, she was a nun with long golden hair and strong-willed crimson eyes. While standing with impeccable posture, she was met by a unified gaze from everyone in the room and seemed uncomfortable. However, with the giant figure in armor standing right behind her, she couldn't retreat either.

Eventually, as if resigning herself, she shook her head and stepped straight into the room—...

**"—Firuore-chan?"**
**"Gyaaah?!"**

The moment she took her first step and spotted Sakura waving her hand towards her, her well-composed expression stiffened dramatically, and her face instantly turned pale.

She stopped her movement, leaning her weight on the forward foot she had put down, but after a moment, she tried to turn her back as if to leave.

**"Firuore, everyone already knows. Come here quietly."**
**"Guh, not just Sakura but Tiga too..."**
**"Of course I'm here. Really, you've gone and done something reckless, haven't you."**

At Tiga's call, shrugging his shoulders as if to say "good grief," Firuore—half-turned—looked pained, but seemed to have resigned herself.

Giving up on fleeing back, she hunched her shoulders dejectedly and approached, took a deep breath naturally in front of the two, Tiga and Sakura, who she obviously knew—and then...

**"Devoting prayers for the kingdom's people is the true purpose of the 'Dragon Faith.' I merely followed its teachings. So there!"**
**"So there' nothing..."**
**"I think scolding and lecturing are unavoidable~"**
**"But I'm not wrong!"**

The bravado she displayed after puffing out her chest and declaring that boldly shattered immediately. At the nun—Firuore—who let out a scream-like voice and held her head, Felt couldn't help but raise an eyebrow.

She was quite a different girl from her initial visual impression and atmosphere.

And also—...

**"She's a fair bit taller than me. Is that the difference from livin' in luxury as a kid?"**
**"Wait a moment, luxury? Are you calling *me* luxurious? If so, that's a huge mistake. The motto of the 'Dragon Faith' is the spirit of charity! Most of the Faith's mercy and charitable spirit is directed outward, not inward. In other words..."**
**"In other words?"**
**"We're pretty much always starving!"**

Placing her hand on her own stomach, Firuore loudly proclaimed the Faith's austere poverty. Scratched her cheek with a finger at Firuore's intensity and volume, Firuore was then hit on the back of the head. The one who hit her head as she turned around saying "Ow!" was Tiga behind her.

Looking at Firuore as if she were a troublesome younger sister, he said,

**"Don't go around loudly announcing our shame. It's a secret we've managed to keep hidden from the country's core until now. You're not planning to expose everything at this rate, are you?"**
**"Th-that couldn't be, right? You all, do you think I have such a loose tongue that..."**
**"Then what about the 'Sacrament,' hmm~?"**
**"————"**

The sound of Firuore gasping at the pursuit echoed oddly loudly in the throne room.

Having her face peered at from both sides by Tiga and Sakura, Firuore opened and closed her mouth a few times before finally saying,

**"...I-I made sure there were no witnesses!"**
**""Sigh..."** (in unison)
**"What a huge sigh! Is this, is this the treatment I get for helping someone? It says so in the scriptures too! 'Evil deeds mingled with the twilight darkness are like a bonfire at noon in the Dragon's eyes. The arrogance of believing you can hide it forever is the self-created darkness of delusion!'"**

Hastily pulling out a thick scripture and holding it aloft with both hands, Firuore made that excuse.

Felt wasn't too familiar with it either, but from the content, she understood it to mean something like, since secrets will be found out anyway, you shouldn't be overconfident about hiding them.

Still, the 'Dragon Faith' scriptures probably wouldn't be happy to be treated as an excuse manual.

**"Sir Knight Marcos, how was it?"**

Setting aside the exchange between the 'Dragon Faith' members, Miklotov directed his question to the Captain of the Royal Guard who had entered the throne room with Firuore.

Called upon and questioned, Marcos, keeping his stern expression, replied with a "Yes,"

**"I accompanied her in person and witnessed the power of Lady Firuore's Sacrament."**
**"'Witnessed,' meaning..."**
**"—The evil lanterns harbored within Duke Crusch Karsten have had their lifelines severed and lost their influence."**

At that report from Marcos, an emotion of unknown answer stiffened Felt's cheek.

Crusch was saved—that was joyous. But, having been saved by the 'Dragon Faith'—that wasn't something she could rejoice over unreservedly.

However, as she had resolved just moments ago, only the people involved could make that choice.

**"—Hmm, I see."**

Accepting a similarly complex sentiment borne of years of experience, similar to the difficult emotion Felt had just tasted, Miklotov glanced at Firuore's state. To Firuore, who looked up at his gaze, Miklotov stroked his long beard and continued, "Now then,"

**"There are many matters we must discuss. Of course, the Sacrament Lady Firuore has demonstrated is one, but more than that..."**
**"—? Was there something else?"**

Tilting her head, Firuore furrowed her brows, looking as if she had no idea what Miklotov was about to say.

Regardless of Firuore's reaction, Miklotov likely hesitated to put the next part into words out of consideration for Felt's presence.

But—

**"Don't need that kinda consideration. —Reinhard."**
**"Yes."**

Saying that, Felt held out her hand, and Reinhard—as if he knew what to do—gently placed *it* into her waiting hand.

Instantly, Felt confirmed the feel of it in her hand, then flicked it forward with her finger—towards Firuore.

**"Wh-Whoa, hey, what's this?"**

Firuore caught the flicked object with both hands she instinctively raised. Blinking at Felt's sudden action, Felt gave a small snort towards her.

And then, to everyone present who was wide-eyed at Felt's action, she shrugged her shoulders—...

**"This is what every single one of you here wanted to see, right?"**

Following the direction Felt indicated with her raised thumb, there was Firuore, who had caught *it* and cupped it in both hands—and within her hands, the badge she had received was glowing brilliantly.

It was precisely the proof of a maiden chosen by the Dragon Jewel. —The vivid, sprouting light that blessed the existence of the sixth maiden recognized by a Dragon Jewel.
